 library. The protocol requires CMakeLists.txt, src/Main.cpp, and tests/test_main.cpp. The architecture should provide workbook-like structures; robust CSV import/export; column selection/filtering; sorting; aggregation; missing/invalid value handling; and deterministic processing reports. Tests must be concise and deterministic, including runtime wiring and a representative rule.\n\nPlanned repository files (6–8 total, exactly listed and consistent):\n1) CMakeLists.txt (build configuration, C++17, one library target, one test target, enable ctest)\n2) src/Main.cpp (runtime entry: small, non-interactive demo wiring the API, reading from string streams, printing a brief result)\n3) tests/test_main.cpp (single protocol-level test: includes headers only; covers CSV import, filter+sort+aggregate behavior with missing values, and report determinism)\n4) include/ExcelCore.h (public API: Workbook, Sheet, Row, Cell types; CSV import/export helpers; header-only declarations with necessary std includes)\n5) src/ExcelCore.cpp (implement sheet storage, cell parsing, CSV read/write with robust quoting, missing handling)\n6) include/ExcelOps.h (public API: column selection, predicates, filtering, sorting, aggregation, ProcessingReport)\n7) src/ExcelOps.cpp (implement filtering, stable sorting with missing ordering policy, aggregations with invalid skipping, report generation)\n\nCanonical public API: ExcelCore::Workbook/Sheet for data model and CSV I/O; ExcelOps functions for processing (selectColumns, filterRows, sortRows, aggregateColumn, buildReport). Runtime and tests use only these headers.\n\nDesign implications: headers are self-contained; sources include algorithm headers; CSV parsing is streaming-based; missing values represented as empty strings or explicit markers; invalid numeric parsing tracked in report; sorting defines deterministic tie-breakers. No network or OS-specific code.",
